# Every fusion, beside whether its layer must move with another. One row per fusion, `violates`
# where the layer moves together with another layer, or is composed from itself.
#
# The definition of a layer, as a rule
#   The rule is `pm:Layer`'s own definition, which the schema also states as one of the model's
#   falsifiers: a layer is a place where a remainder can be held independently of every other
#   layer, and if two candidate layers' remainders always move together, they were never two
#   layers. If A is composed from B while B is composed from A, no change to one leaves the other
#   where it was. The pair fails the definition: the two layers are one.
#
# The repair is a merge, not a deleted edge
#   A rule that said "break the loop" would point at denying a real supply relation. What the
#   schema asks for is merging the layers, or one filer withdrawing a part, and that is what the
#   detail names.
#
# It sees what no other rule here sees
#   A loop inside one document is also caught by a rule confined to one document, though at the
#   top of the composition and pointing at the wrong repair. A loop across documents whose factors
#   close round it is reached by no local rule at all, and `conversion_cycle_does_not_close`
#   rightly ignores it because the units do close. That case is two layers moving together with no
#   arithmetic error anywhere, and nothing else here reaches it.
#
# The population is the fusions, exactly
#   To sit on a loop a layer must be composed from something.
#
# One row per fusion, not one per pair
#   For two layers composed from each other the pairs hold (A, A) and (A, B), so joining them
#   directly reports one defect twice. A rule's grain is its subject's grain, and the subject here
#   is the layer.
#
# A layer composed from itself is the simplest violation
#   It is also the easiest to exclude by accident. Writing `AND NOT (co_moves_with = self)` so the
#   detail does not read "`compute` moves with `compute`" would make the rule never fire on a layer
#   composed from itself, the purest case of a thing that is not a layer.
#   `composition/descent.sqlc` gives (A, A) only when A really reaches A, so that pair here is never
#   trivial. The detail branches on it instead.
#
# Probe
#   Inside a transaction you roll back, close a loop within one document, then a loop across
#   documents whose factors close round it. This rule must fire on both, and
#   `conversion_cycle_does_not_close` must stay quiet on the second.
-- composition/descent.sqlc against itself reversed; conformance rule "layers that always move
-- together are one layer".
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT f.filing, f.layer,
           c.filing IS NOT NULL AS violates,
           CASE WHEN c.filing IS NULL
                THEN format('`%s` holds its remainder independently', f.layer)
                WHEN c.partner IS NULL
                THEN format('`%s` is composed from itself, so its figure depends on its own value '
                            'and there is no order to work it out in', f.layer)
                ELSE format('`%s` moves with `%s`, and %s layers here are one layer: the repair '
                            'is to merge them, not to break a part',
                            f.layer, c.partner, c.members)
           END AS detail
    FROM      (
        :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
    ) f
    LEFT JOIN (
        SELECT m.filing, m.layer, m.class, m.members,
               bool_or(m.co_moves_with_filing = m.filing AND m.co_moves_with_layer = m.layer)
                 AS reaches_itself,
               min(m.co_moves_with_filing || '/' || m.co_moves_with_layer)
                 FILTER (WHERE NOT (m.co_moves_with_filing = m.filing
                                AND m.co_moves_with_layer = m.layer)) AS partner
        FROM (
            :compose(rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc)
        ) m
        GROUP BY m.filing, m.layer, m.class, m.members
    ) c ON c.filing = f.filing AND c.layer = f.layer
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'layers_move_together'
